Weekly Prompt - 
Pick a S.O.L.I.D. design principle and explain the concept and how you can meet that principle when designing a program

The Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) requires that a class should have one job and one reason to 
change.  When a class has multiple tasks, it becomes more confusing and harder to test. If changes are 
required later (after testing or additional requirements), if SRP has been followed, the changes will 
be clear, and there is less of a chance of unintentionally changing functionality.  If a class starts 
to have more than one job, it violates SRP and should be split into two or more classes.  If you have 
a class called Cars that prints car information, stores car information, and modifies car information, 
that is too much for the class; it should be split into three classes: CarsPrint, Cars, and CarsModify.  
This separation keeps each class individualized, easier to maintain, and less likely to introduce 
issues when updated. This is not specific to classes; SRP applies to other units of code such as 
functions, modules, and packages.  

